From 890c06117a5f93d7eff8df73953ff3769950baca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wim Taymans <wtaymans@redhat.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Apr 2026 18:19:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] security: fix integer overflow in port latency offset
 conversion

Client-supplied int64_t offset was multiplied by 1000 without overflow
check. Use spa_overflow_mul to detect and reject values that would
overflow.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Note (backport to 1.6.4): spa/utils/overflow.h and the spa_overflow_mul
macro do not exist in this series (they were added upstream by a separate
SPA commit after 1.6.4), so use __builtin_mul_overflow directly, which is
exactly what spa_overflow_mul expands to upstream. The include line of
the original commit is dropped accordingly.

diff --git a/src/modules/module-protocol-pulse/pulse-server.c b/src/modules/module-protocol-pulse/pulse-server.c
index 59610ef..d9dc471 100644
--- a/src/modules/module-protocol-pulse/pulse-server.c
+++ b/src/modules/module-protocol-pulse/pulse-server.c
@@ -3161,7 +3161,8 @@ static int do_set_port_latency_offset(struct client *client, uint32_t command, u
 	if (port_name == NULL)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	value = offset * 1000;  /* to nsec */
+	if (__builtin_mul_overflow(offset, (int64_t)1000, &value))
+		return -EINVAL;
 
 	if ((card = select_object(manager, &sel)) == NULL)
 		return -ENOENT;
